<figure class="wire-study" aria-labelledby="wire-study-title">
  <figcaption id="wire-study-title">
    <span>FIELD MAP / ORDINARY MEMORY</span>
    <strong>One packed record, three independent fields</strong>
  </figcaption>
  <p class="direction">Offsets increase from left to right. Read the numbered field groups in order.</p>
  <ol class="fields">
    <li>
      <span class="label">Tag · 1 byte</span>
      <div class="bytes"><span><small>+0</small><code>a5</code></span></div>
      <p>Keep <code>0xa5</code> as a byte.</p>
    </li>
    <li>
      <span class="label">Length · LE16</span>
      <div class="bytes">
        <span><small>+1</small><code>34</code></span><span><small>+2</small><code>12</code></span>
      </div>
      <p>Decode <code>0x1234</code>.</p>
    </li>
    <li>
      <span class="label">Code · BE16</span>
      <div class="bytes">
        <span><small>+3</small><code>ab</code></span><span><small>+4</small><code>cd</code></span>
      </div>
      <p>Decode <code>0xabcd</code>.</p>
    </li>
  </ol>
  <p class="sequence"><strong>Check the span</strong> (5 bytes here) <span aria-hidden="true">→</span> <strong>Decode each field</strong> by its declared order <span aria-hidden="true">→</span> <strong>Use numeric values</strong> on either host.</p>
  <p class="note">If the buffer begins at an even address, both 16-bit fields begin at odd addresses. Use a bytewise decoder or a suitable unaligned helper, not a native pointer cast. Re-encode each field separately when writing the record.</p>
</figure>

